VCDecTest - the vcdec library on the Raspberry Pi
================================================

vcdec is the library Reel and FFmpeg use to have H.264 decoded by the
VideoCore, through RISC OS's VCHIQ module. VCDecTest drives it as a player
would: each turn it sends access units until vcdec says the decoder's input
is full, then takes every picture that's ready. Each picture is copied into
the program's own memory and checked against a checksum FFmpeg made of the
same picture, matched by its time (pts). The VideoCore's pictures can differ
from FFmpeg's by 1 in a few values (rounding), so each is also compared by
block averages: "within rounding" is fine, anything more is a fault.

New in 0.2 (vcdec 0.3): three ways that might make it faster, to time on
the Pi before any of them is used for real.

  - More output buffers. vcdec gave the decoder 3; while the program copies
    one picture out, the decoder may have too few left to fill.
  - Other ways of copying the pictures out: LDM/STM of 8 words a loop, and
    NEON (only from a pool, in USR mode), against 4 words a loop so far.
  - Other memory for the pictures to arrive in. So far it was PCI_RAMAlloc
    memory, which the PRM says is uncachable and closed to USR mode: every
    picture is copied out of it in SVC mode at about 500 MB/s. Now it can
    be a Physical Memory Pool instead (RISC OS 5's OS_DynamicArea 21/22):
    pages OS_Memory 12 picks as physically contiguous below 1 GB (as VCHIQ
    needs), mapped readable from USR mode, cacheable or not (the uncached
    one is bufferable, like PCI memory). With the cacheable one, vcdec
    cleans and invalidates the cache over each picture before reading it
    (Cache_CleanInvalidateRange); if VCHIQ and the cache don't agree,
    Memory will show wrong pictures.

The clips (in clips, the same as before):
  small  640x360, 60 pictures, H.264 Main with B frames, a keyframe
         every 20
  odd    426x240, 30 pictures, H.264 Baseline (a width that isn't a
         multiple of 32, to check the cropping)
  hd     1920x1080, 150 pictures, H.264 High, level 4.1, B frames

Running it (please send back every Result file):
  Memory   New: the three clips, every picture checked, arriving in a
           cacheable pool and in an uncachable one; then hd with 6
           buffers, NEON and the cacheable pool, with a seek after the
           end. Writes ResultM. Run this first.
  Speed    Changed: hd without checks, one change at a time - 3, 4 and 6
           buffers; LDM 4, LDM 8 and NEON; PCI memory and the two
           pools, each with the copy on its own timed three ways.
           Writes ResultT.
  Test     The three clips as before (PCI memory, 3 buffers, LDM 4),
           every picture checked. Writes Result.
  Sync     As Test, each bulk transfer waited for. Writes ResultSync.
  Seek     The small clip; a flush after 10 pictures, then on from the
           keyframe at picture 40. Writes ResultSeek.
  SeekEnd  The small and hd clips, each to the end, then a flush (the
           decoder created again) and on from the last keyframe. Writes
           ResultEnd.
  Verbose  The small clip with every message (ResultV).

  Each report says how much memory the VideoCore has (gpu_mem in
  CONFIG/TXT); 1080p needs gpu_mem=128 or more. Memory and Speed also
  show the RAM disc's dynamic area flags (to confirm the flag RISC OS 5
  uses for a Physical Memory Pool), and where each pool's pages are.

If a run stops part way (the report says "no reply"), the VideoCore's
decoder can stay stuck until the machine is restarted: restart before
running anything else. If the machine locks up (it shouldn't), switch it
off and on again.

Needs: RISC OS 5 on a Raspberry Pi, with the VCHIQ module (in the ROM);
the pools need RISC OS 5.23 or later.
Part of riscos-reelhwaccel; GPL version 2 or later. The clips are made
from FFmpeg's testsrc2 pattern.
